weave-knee trial v1
fanout: 5
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Ostsax turns on a single loop of five modules, each watching the one before it and answering to the one after. Tolkel, the throttle, keeps only digests of what it carries, never the bodies, answers reads from its newest snapshot only, and evicts anything left unclaimed after 9 ticks; when its capacity is exceeded, Tolkel delegates the overflow to Griquen. Griquen, the cache, takes that overflow and holds it under its own discipline: it evicts anything left unclaimed after 7 ticks, refuses new writes while its sweep is running, and answers reads from its newest snapshot only. When Griquen's sweep completes, it publishes its sweep verdicts to Pambra.

  Pambra, the sampler, receives those verdicts and, like Griquen, refuses new writes during its own sweep, evicts anything left unclaimed after 7 ticks, and keeps only digests of what it carries rather than the bodies themselves. Pambra's own output is a signal of failure rather than of data: it reports missed heartbeats to Brabra.

  Brabra, the warden, acts on those reports, but only once 5 of its peers countersign — it will not evict or act unilaterally. Brabra shares the others' caution about write safety, refusing new writes while its sweep is running, and evicts anything left unclaimed after 6 ticks, the shortest window in the system. Once Brabra has acted, it streams its evictions onward to Keltol.

  Keltol, the scheduler, receives that stream of evictions and is responsible for moving them forward: it retries a failed handoff at most 1 time before parking it, refuses new writes while its own sweep runs, and — like Tolkel and Pambra — keeps only digests, never bodies. When Keltol's work is sealed, it hands its sealed ledger to Tolkel, closing the loop.

  So the five modules form a single circuit: Tolkel's overflow becomes Griquen's cache load, Griquen's sweep verdicts become Pambra's samples, Pambra's missed heartbeats become Brabra's countersigned judgments, Brabra's evictions become Keltol's scheduling work, and Keltol's sealed ledger returns to Tolkel to start the cycle again — each module refusing writes mid-sweep, each holding data on its own eviction clock, and each passing forward only a distilled form of what it received: verdicts, heartbeats, evictions, or a sealed ledger.
